        "pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln1179-1219"
      ],
      "reply": "The sentence is:\n\n“I—I’m a little girl,” said Alice, rather doubtfully, as she remembered\nthe number of changes she had gone through that day.\n\nWhat makes it striking is that naming herself doesn’t instantly make the identity feel secure. She can say the words, but the hesitation remains because her recent experience has made the label feel unstable—and someone else is actively challenging it.\n\nThat may be part of why “manager” feels awkward for you too. A new role can be real before it feels familiar; saying the title aloud can sound like a claim you still have to grow into, or one you expect someone else to question. You might try treating it less as a declaration that you’ve become a different kind of person and more as a simple description of what you’re doing now: “I’m managing…” often feels gentler than “I am a manager.”",
