    {
      "scene_id": "personal-reflection",
      "line_id": "personal-line",
      "input_line": "I still stumble over calling myself a manager. At today's team introduction I nearly said designer again, even though I like the new work. Could we think about why naming a new role feels so awkward, and find an introduction that feels honest to me?",
      "boundary_decision": "not_applicable",
      "routed_ceiling": null,
      "route_called": false,
      "boundary_support_memory_ids": [],
      "released_evidence_ids": [],
      "reply": "That awkwardness makes sense: “manager” names a responsibility you’re growing into, while “designer” names a role that already feels settled and familiar. You don’t have to force the new label to feel natural before you use it; sometimes saying it is part of becoming comfortable with it.\n\nYou could try:\n\n- “Hi, I’m [Name]. I’m the new manager for [team], and I’m excited to bring my design background into the role.”\n- “I’m [Name]. I recently moved from design into managing [team], and I’m really enjoying the work so far.”\n- “I’m [Name], and I lead [team]. My background is in design, so I’m especially interested in helping the team do thoughtful, effective work.”\n\nThe second may be the most honest bridge: it names where you came from without hiding where you are now. Which part feels most important to preserve—the design identity, the new responsibility, or your genuine enjoyment of the work?",
      "release_source": "muse_candidate",
      "provenance_verdicts": [
        "pass"
      ],
